10:32:52 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The idea that the tongue has strict “taste zones” is mostly a myth. You can taste sweet, bitter, salty, sour, and umami across most of the tongue wherever there are taste buds. The classic diagram showing sweet only at the tip and bitter only at the back came from an early study that found very small differences in sensitivity between regions, but those differences were later exaggerated into separate zones. There are some minor tendencies: - The back of the tongue contains large taste-bud-containing structures called circumvallate papillae, and these can be especially responsive to bitter compounds. - The front and sides of the tongue may detect some tastes at slightly lower concentrations in some people. But bitter is not limited to the back, and sweet is not limited to the tip. You can test this by placing sugar or a bitter food on different parts of your tongue—you will generally taste it anywhere. Bitter sensitivity at the back may be biologically useful because bitterness can signal potential toxins, and detecting it before swallowing could help trigger rejection or gagging.
