10:33:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It was taught for ages because it was based on a real observation that got distorted over time—not because teachers invented it from nothing. In 1901, German scientist David Hänig measured how sensitive different parts of the tongue were to sweet, sour, salty, and bitter substances. He found *small relative differences*: for example, one region might need a slightly lower concentration of a substance before a person noticed it. Later, a textbook-style illustration appears to have converted those subtle differences into a dramatic “map” with separate regions: sweet at the tip, salty at the front sides, sour farther back, and bitter only at the back. That version was easy to memorize, so it spread through school materials. The important correction is: - **All basic tastes can be detected over much of the tongue.** - **Different regions can have modest sensitivity differences**, and individuals differ. - **The back of the tongue does have many large papillae with taste buds and is often quite sensitive to bitter compounds.** - But there are no hard boundaries where, for example, the tip “cannot taste bitter” or the back “cannot taste sweet.” So the school tongue map was an oversimplification of a genuine experiment, presented as if it showed exclusive taste territories.
