10:33:15 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Your experience can be real without the old “exclusive-zone” map being correct. The key distinction is between **“only tastes bitter at the back”** and **“I am much more sensitive to bitterness at the back.”** The first is false for most people; the second can absolutely be true. A few reasons bitter medicines often seem strongest toward the back: - **They often dissolve and spread backward in saliva.** When you swallow a pill or liquid medicine, it tends to wash over the back of the tongue and throat, where it may linger. - **There are many taste buds in the large papillae near the back of the tongue.** Those areas can contribute strongly to bitterness, and some people may be especially sensitive there. - **People vary.** Taste receptor genes, the number and distribution of taste buds, saliva flow, and prior experience all affect where and how intensely tastes are perceived. - **The “back of the mouth” sensation can include more than taste.** Bitter medicines may cause dryness, irritation, or an unpleasant aftertaste, and odors traveling up from the throat into the nose can amplify the experience. - **Your attention is drawn there during swallowing.** Bitter compounds at the back may be especially noticeable because that is the last area they contact before swallowing. So the correction to the tongue map is not “every part of the tongue tastes exactly the same.” It is: **there are no sharp, separate territories.** Sweetness and bitterness can be sensed in multiple tongue regions, but sensitivity can vary by location and by person—and for you, bitterness may indeed be markedly stronger at the back.
