10:33:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s a reasonable analogy: biology often does use regional specialization, and the retina is a good example. The difference is that the tongue’s specialization is **not organized into clean one-taste-per-region zones** the way the retina has a cone-rich center and rod-rich periphery. The tongue does have different structures in different places: - **Front:** mostly fungiform papillae - **Sides/back:** foliate papillae - **Far back:** large circumvallate papillae, with many taste buds Those regions differ in taste-bud density, nerve supply, and probably somewhat in receptor expression and sensitivity. The far-back circumvallate area can contribute strongly to bitter perception, so “bitter tends to feel stronger at the back” is biologically plausible and often true. But within a taste bud, there are cells capable of responding to different taste categories—sweet, bitter, sour, salty, and umami—and bitter-responsive cells and receptors are not confined to the back. Put a bitter substance on the tip and, assuming enough concentration and contact time, you can taste bitterness there too. Likewise, sweetness is detectable at the back. So the accurate comparison would be: - **Retina:** strong, well-defined regional specialization for different visual tasks. - **Tongue:** real regional differences in anatomy and sensitivity, but broad overlap in the ability to detect each basic taste. The old map took a modest tendency—especially the prominence of bitter sensation at the back—and turned it into sharply bordered “sweet zone,” “bitter zone,” and so on. Your intuition that anatomy can make the back especially salient for bitter taste is sound; the strict map is the overreach.
