10:36:38 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It’s reasonable to feel wary when a familiar school diagram turns out to be oversimplified. But the lesson is not that school science is untrustworthy; it is that science teaching often starts with **useful models**, then adds exceptions and detail later. A diagram can be: - **Basically reliable:** major bones, organs, blood circulation, cell structure. - **Simplified but useful:** basic food groups, “five senses,” atom diagrams, the tongue map. - **Outdated in its exact form:** the old Food Pyramid, which has been replaced in many places by plate-based guides such as MyPlate or similar national guidance. The tongue map is a good example of how to read school diagrams: trust the broad concept—taste buds are distributed in different tongue structures, and regions can differ—but do not assume a colorful boundary line means literal, exclusive anatomy. Science is not asking you to trust every classroom drawing as permanent final truth. It is more like: trust conclusions in proportion to the evidence, and expect introductory lessons to trade precision for clarity. For anatomy, the core claims are usually very robust because they can be directly observed and repeatedly tested. For nutrition, recommendations change more often because they involve population studies, changing diets, and practical policy choices. For things like the tongue map, the broad phenomenon was real but the memorable simplified version overstated it. So the healthy response is not “discard everything from school.” It is: > Treat basic school material as a solid starting framework, then update particular details when better evidence or better explanations become available.
