10:37:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That simple fact is not quite right: **we do not have evidence that particular tongue regions contain “way more taste buds for a particular taste” in the classic map pattern.** Different regions do have different kinds and numbers of papillae and taste buds—for example, the large circumvallate papillae at the back contain many taste buds. But those taste buds are not simply “bitter buds,” while tip taste buds are “sweet buds.” Taste-responsive cell types are broadly distributed and mixed within taste-bud-containing regions. So: - **True:** the tongue is anatomically nonuniform; some areas have more taste buds and different nerve supply. - **True:** the back can be a major site for bitter sensations, especially in real eating and swallowing. - **Not established:** each region has a much larger dedicated population for one taste, making it “in charge” of that taste in the classic sweet-tip/salty-side/bitter-back arrangement. Overlap and variation are not just distracting exceptions—they are why the familiar map does not reliably predict where a person will detect or experience each taste most strongly. The cleanest accurate rule is: **taste is distributed, with regional differences, rather than partitioned into dedicated territories.**
