10:38:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: No. Something being widely taught—even “officially” in curricula—means it has been accepted as a teaching model, not that it is guaranteed to be permanently correct. “Established fact” in science means a claim is strongly supported by reproducible evidence, not merely that many schools repeat it. Curricula and textbooks can lag behind research, preserve oversimplifications, or use diagrams that are useful for beginners but misleading if read literally. For the tongue map, the evidence-supported position is: - Taste buds and taste-responsive cells are distributed across multiple tongue regions. - All basic tastes can generally be detected from multiple regions. - The classic map’s sharp zones and its specific one-taste-per-area pattern are not a reliable anatomical fact. - Regional anatomical and sensitivity differences do exist, which is why the old diagram was not completely arbitrary. So widespread teaching explains why it feels established; it does not settle the scientific question by itself.
