10:38:48 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is definitely a nicer, more memorable story. Biology often *does* have tidy divisions of labor, which is why the tongue map feels so satisfying. But the more accurate picture is not “everything is identical everywhere.” It is: - different tongue regions have different structures and nerve inputs; - taste buds contain different receptor-cell types; - those receptor types are broadly distributed rather than arranged in four clean territories; - the brain combines signals from across the mouth into one taste experience. That is still a division of labor—just at the level of **cell types, receptors, nerves, and brain processing**, not a color-coded geographic map of the tongue. So it is fine to use the old map as a loose mnemonic if you remember its limits. It just should not be treated as literal anatomy.
