10:39:01 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is not different in principle. Just as fingertips and palms differ in touch sensitivity, different parts of the tongue can differ in taste sensitivity because they differ in papillae, taste-bud density, nerves, and how food contacts them. What is disputed is the **specific classic pattern** and its neatness. With touch, we can reliably say, “fingertips have much finer spatial resolution than the palm.” That is a strong, consistent anatomical and functional difference. With taste, the comparable accurate statement is more like: > Tongue regions are not identical; they may differ in detection thresholds and in how strongly they contribute to a taste experience. But it is not similarly well supported to say, as a universal rule: > The tip is the sweet-specialist region, the sides are the salty/sour-specialist regions, and the back is the bitter-specialist region. The tongue has real regional specialization, just not a clean, one-quality-per-region layout. Your fingertip analogy supports the idea that location can matter—not the conclusion that the old school map’s particular labels and borders are established facts.
